---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **파는 조건.** (`/terms`)
 *
 * ── 왜 (2번 지시 13:3x) ───────────────────────────────────────
 * 「/terms · /refund 가 404 입니다. 6번에게 건 글의 내용을 K콘텐츠에 맞춰 쓰십시오.
 *   ⚠ 5번 것은 **「투자 판단」이 아니라 「시청·흥행 예측을 제공하지 않는다」**입니다」
 *
 * ⛔ 6번 글을 그대로 베끼지 않는다. 파는 것이 다르다 —
 *    서울마켓은 시세이고 우리는 **시청·차트·관심도**다. 안 하는 약속이 다르면 약관도 달라야 한다.
 * ⛔ 「SeoulMarkets」·「백년지도」·「KLifeMap」이라는 말이 여기 남으면 안 된다(복사하면 남는다).
 * ⛔ 값을 안 적는다. 사장님 손이다.
 * ⛔ 넷플릭스 주간 순위의 재배포 조건이 대장에 ⬜ 미확인이라, **그 표는 아직 라이선스에 안 넣는다.**
 *    못 한 확인을 한 것처럼 적는 것이 이 지면에서 제일 나쁘다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import { PUBLISHER } from '../../consts';
import { 한벌 } from '../../data/wikitip-bundle';

const MAIL = 'cs@klifedesign.net';
const 벌 = 한벌();
const TITLE = 'Terms of the data licence';
const DESC = 'What you may do with our tables, where the figures come from, and the one thing we do '
  + 'not sell: a prediction of what will be watched.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  url: 'https://www.kculturewire.com/terms',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'K Culture Wire', url: 'https://www.kculturewire.com' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: PUBLISHER.legalName, email: MAIL },
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd} canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/terms">
  <h1>Terms of the data licence</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Plain terms for a small product: {벌.files} tables, {벌.rows.toLocaleString()} rows, and one
    promise we will not make.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>What you license</h2>
    <p>
      A licence to use the tables inside your organisation — analysis, internal reporting, research,
      and charts in your own work with the source named. One licence covers one organisation.
    </p>
    <p>
      You may quote figures publicly if you name <b>K Culture Wire</b> and link the page you took
      them from. We would rather be quoted than hoarded.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What you may not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Do not resell the tables</b>, in whole or repackaged, as a dataset of their own.</li>
      <li><b>Do not redistribute them publicly</b> as files. Quoting figures is fine; posting the CSV is not.</li>
      <li>Do not present the numbers as your own measurement. They are ours, and our corrections record is part of what you are buying.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
    <p>
      Every table is built from sources that publish openly — Netflix's own weekly Top 10 lists,
      Wikimedia's pageview API, Wikidata, Korea's national statistics service and companies' own
      annual filings. We name the source on every page and in the column dictionary.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>One table is not licensed yet.</b> The redistribution terms for the streaming chart source
      are still being confirmed, so the chart-derived tables are not part of any licence we grant
      today. We check a licence before we publish, not after. The other tables do not wait on it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>We do not sell a prediction of what will be watched</h2>
    <p>
      This is the important one. Our tables say <b>what has already been counted</b>: which titles
      reached a chart, in how many countries, for how many weeks, and how often a name was looked up.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>They are not a forecast of viewing, of box office, or of whether a title will succeed.</b>{' '}
      We publish no such model and make no such claim. If you use these figures to decide what to
      commission, license or acquire, that judgement is yours — and the tables will not tell you what
      is missing from your question.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      We also do not sell audience demographics, individual viewing behaviour, or anything that
      identifies a person. None of our sources contain it and we have not inferred it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Accuracy, and what we do when we are wrong</h2>
    <p>
      We publish every figure we have had to change, with the old value beside the new one and the
      cause it came from — <a href="/corrections">the corrections record</a> ships with the data and
      is a file you can read before you buy. If a figure in a table you licensed turns out to be
      wrong, tell us and we will correct it, tell everyone else who has it, and refund you under{' '}
      <a href="/refund">the refund policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Data can be complete, current and still not answer your question. We will tell you when it
      cannot, before you pay, and we consider that reply part of what we sell.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Liability, in one paragraph</h2>
    <p>
      We stand behind the accuracy of the figures and refund them when they are wrong. Beyond that
      refund we are not liable for decisions taken using them — see the section above about what we
      do not sell. Nothing here removes any right you have under Korean consumer law.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Who you are contracting with</h2>
    <p>
      {PUBLISHER.legalName}. Questions about these terms go to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${MAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('K Culture Wire — terms')}`}>{MAIL}</a>,
      and a person answers within a working day.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="back">
    <a href="/data">What is in the tables</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="/refund">Refund policy</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="/privacy">Cookies and what we log</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="/">Front page</a>
  </p>
</WikiTip>

<style>
  h1 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 5vw, 2.2rem); line-height: 1.25; margin: 1.2rem 0 .8rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.02rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 2.5rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  section { margin: 0 0 2.4rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 .6rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  p { font-size: .93rem; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0 0 .9rem; }
  .note { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); }
  ul { padding-left: 1.15rem; font-size: .93rem; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0; }
  li { margin: 0 0 .45rem; }
  .back { margin-top: 3rem; font-size: .9rem; }
  .back a { color: var(--muted); }
</style>
